+/* hero-exit-scroll-timeline: the hero exit (--exit-opacity / --exit-scatter, written per frame by JS
+   in the baseline) as CSS scroll-driven animations on the `--hero-exit` view timeline of .hero-track.
+   Range `contain 0% contain 100%` = the JS formula p = clamp(-top, 0, h - innerHeight) / (h - innerHeight).
+   Only where the JS switched to CSS mode: on WebKit (GestureEvent) with scroll-driven animations at the
+   phone layout (width <= 600px), once the entrance is over (d() in 2.DyWL8TcE.js), the track gets .he-on
+   (parked: the cue's bob moves to .he-bob, the exit is the baseline's own rules from the inline
+   --exit-opacity the JS writes at rest), and .he-css while the page scrolls (live: these animations; the JS
+   writes nothing per frame). Parking at rest matters because WebKit invalidates the style of every element
+   with a relevant scroll-driven animation on every rendering update, moving or not (WebKit source, see the
+   JS comment). Wider layouts (desktop orbit cards: desktop
+   and iPad Safari, a landscape phone) keep the baseline writer: there WebKit drew the animated tilted cards
+   up to 108 levels off the baseline's static draw of the same computed values (width 980, Simulator,
+   OBSERVED; residual R5). Every other engine keeps the baseline writer and none of these rules apply.
+   Every exit animation starts at contain 0.05% (s) with fill forwards, so at rest at the top of the page
+   none is active and the hero is drawn exactly as in the baseline (the baseline's own rules: blur(0px) on
+   the copy and the cue, no filter on the float). For the first frame after .he-css arrives (the new
+   animations are pending) the JS writes the baseline's inline --exit-opacity, so the base values are the
+   exited ones there too; the JS then leaves it (stale) while live, so every live property that reads it is
+   animated (cards, copy, float) or, on the cue, replaced by its value at --exit-opacity 1 (the float carries
+   the cue's exit, and its base values carry it for the pending frame). The
+   `from` keyframes are the baseline value at s (a + (b - a) * s), so inside the range the value is the
+   baseline's a + (b - a) * p.
+   The copy's shift is a separate transform animation, present only while live (a transform animation on
+   .hero-copy, even inactive, made WebKit repaint the title's script accent about 1.4x as often; Simulator,
+   OBSERVED).
+   The scroll cue's own entrance animates --copy-in (in its opacity and transform), so its exit
+   (fade, shift, blur) runs on its only child, .scroll-float (blur on the cue itself over a composited
+   child drew a wider halo in WebKit; while live the cue draws blur(0px), as the baseline rule at
+   --exit-opacity 1), and the float's 2.2 s bob moves to .he-bob, a span the JS wraps
+   around the float's two children in CSS mode (one layer for both, as the baseline's float): an element
+   with both the infinite bob and scroll-driven animations made WebKit recalculate style every frame at
+   rest, and the bob on the two children separately drew the cue 1-2 levels off (Simulator, OBSERVED).
+   opacity/transform/translate animations are separate from the blur ones (compositable); past the end
+   the blur keeps blur(18px) like the baseline, so no filter change when the track reaches its end
